@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy Policy";
}

<div class="saps-content-header">
    <h1 class="h3 mb-0">Privacy Policy</h1>
    <div class="saps-breadcrumb">Home / Privacy</div>
</div>

<p class="text-muted mb-4">
    Last updated: @DateTime.Now.ToString("d MMMM yyyy").
    This policy explains what personal information the SAPS Crime Pre-Reporting System collects, why, who can see
    it, and the rights you have over it under the Protection of Personal Information Act, 2013 (POPIA).
</p>

<div class="card saps-card mb-4">
    <div class="saps-panel-header"><span><i class="bi bi-info-circle"></i> 1. What This System Is</span></div>
    <div class="p-4">
        <p class="mb-0">
            The SAPS Crime Pre-Reporting System lets a member of the public submit a preliminary record of a
            crime before it is opened as an official docket at a police station, and lets SAPS personnel review,
            process, and escalate that record. This system is <strong>not an emergency service</strong> — if
            anyone is in immediate danger, contact your local emergency services directly instead of using this
            site.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card saps-card mb-4">
    <div class="saps-panel-header"><span><i class="bi bi-collection"></i> 2. Information We Collect</span></div>
    <div class="p-4">
        <h2 class="h6">Account information</h2>
        <p>When you register or an administrator creates an account for you, we collect your full name, South
            African ID number, email address, phone number, and a password (which we never store — see
            "How We Protect Your Information" below).</p>

        <h2 class="h6">Report information</h2>
        <p>When you submit a preliminary report, we collect the incident type, description, date, the location
            you describe in text, and — with your device's permission — the GPS coordinates of your current
            location. Those coordinates are used only to find and route your report to the nearest real SAPS
            station, using OpenStreetMap's public station data and geocoding services (Overpass and Nominatim).
            This means your coordinates are sent to OpenStreetMap's servers as part of that lookup, in addition
            to our own.</p>

        <h2 class="h6">Usage and security information</h2>
        <p class="mb-0">We record login timestamps, the IP address associated with certain actions, and an audit
            trail entry for sensitive actions (registrations, logins, docket changes, escalations, referrals, and
            user/account management) so that every change to a case can be traced back to who made it and when.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card saps-card mb-4">
    <div class="saps-panel-header"><span><i class="bi bi-bullseye"></i> 3. Why We Collect It</span></div>
    <div class="p-4">
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li>To verify who you are and restrict your account to the correct role and permissions.</li>
            <li>To route your preliminary report to the SAPS station nearest to where the incident was reported.</li>
            <li>To let you and SAPS personnel track a case from initial report through docket, referral, and escalation.</li>
            <li>To notify you of status changes to your report, docket, or escalation.</li>
            <li>To maintain the audit trail SAPS is required to keep for case handling and accountability.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card saps-card mb-4">
    <div class="saps-panel-header"><span><i class="bi bi-people"></i> 4. Who Can See Your Information</span></div>
    <div class="p-4">
        <p>Access is restricted by role, and every access is logged:</p>
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li><strong>Complainants</strong> can see only their own reports, dockets, escalations, and notifications.</li>
            <li><strong>SAPS Officers</strong> can see preliminary reports and dockets at their assigned station.</li>
            <li><strong>Station Commanders</strong> can see dockets and escalations at their assigned station.</li>
            <li><strong>System Administrators</strong> manage user accounts and stations, but do not need or
                routinely access case content to do so.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card saps-card mb-4">
    <div class="saps-panel-header"><span><i class="bi bi-shield-lock"></i> 5. How We Protect Your Information</span></div>
    <div class="p-4">
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li>Passwords are hashed with bcrypt before storage — we cannot see or recover your actual password,
                and a password reset always requires proving control of your account's email.</li>
            <li>All traffic to this site is encrypted in transit (HTTPS).</li>
            <li>Accounts are temporarily locked after repeated failed login attempts.</li>
            <li>Every sensitive action is written to an audit log that administrators can review but not alter
                through the application.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card saps-card mb-4">
    <div class="saps-panel-header"><span><i class="bi bi-clock-history"></i> 6. How Long We Keep It</span></div>
    <div class="p-4 mb-0">
        <p class="mb-0">
            We retain your account and case information for as long as your account is active and your case
            remains open, and thereafter for as long as SAPS's record-keeping obligations under South African law
            require. Deactivating an account does not delete the case history associated with it, since that
            history may itself form part of an official record.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card saps-card mb-4">
    <div class="saps-panel-header"><span><i class="bi bi-file-earmark-check"></i> 7. Your Rights Under POPIA</span></div>
    <div class="p-4">
        <p>Under the Protection of Personal Information Act, 2013, you have the right to:</p>
        <ul class="mb-3">
            <li>Ask what personal information we hold about you and request a copy of it.</li>
            <li>Ask us to correct personal information that is inaccurate or out of date.</li>
            <li>Object to how your information is processed, or ask us to delete it — subject to our legal
                obligation to retain official case records.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-0">To exercise any of these rights, contact your system administrator through the details below.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card saps-card mb-4">
    <div class="saps-panel-header"><span><i class="bi bi-cookie"></i> 8. Cookies</span></div>
    <div class="p-4 mb-0">
        <p class="mb-0">
            This site uses a session cookie to keep you signed in, and stores your light/dark theme preference in
            your browser's local storage. We do not use third-party advertising or tracking cookies.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card saps-card mb-4">
    <div class="saps-panel-header"><span><i class="bi bi-envelope"></i> 9. Contact Us</span></div>
    <div class="p-4 mb-0">
        <p class="mb-0">
            Questions about this policy, or requests relating to your personal information, can be directed to
            your station's System Administrator, or to <a href="mailto:privacy@saps.local">privacy@saps.local</a>.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<p class="text-muted small">
    We may update this policy from time to time as the system changes. Material changes will be reflected in the
    "Last updated" date above.
</p>
